A||||6||RFMike||I need help fast||||1
Z||000000||RFMike||11-16-1999||10:21 PM||||Today I took my 86 GT to Tires Plus for new skins.  I told them to align it, and the service manager came back and told me that they would have to remove the upper ball joints and rotate them 180 degrees in order to make the corrections.  Same with the lowers.  Is this straight, or bs?  If its true, how much extra time should this job take, over and above a more typical alignment?  I need some knowledge before I talk to these guys again, so any help would be handy and appreciated.<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||11-16-1999||10:33 PM||||My fiero's front end was too far out of alignment to be fixed by rotating the ball joints 180 degrees. He told me that you could get 1/2 of a degree with the ball joints. At the body shop, we use one of those cheap Craftsman magnetic "angle finder" gauges to stick on the rotor to check the angle while making frame repairs. If your tires are wearing out on the inside pretty bad, then your alignment guy might be right. I would would try the angle gauge before I spent too much money having ball joints swapped. If they do have to be removed, go ahead and replace them with new ones- NOT TRW!!!!! they are junk.||||reg||1
Z||000002||RFMike||11-16-1999||10:45 PM||||They thought they could bring it into spec by rotating.  The existing balljoints are tight- there is absolutely no play in the wheels when it is on the rack.  I don't know if these are original or not; if they seem ok, do you think I should go ahead and replace them anyway?  This was something I was contemplating this winter, after reading some of the stories about balljoints falling apart.  I wasn't ready to do it right now, after shelling out for tires.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||11-17-1999||12:52 AM||||RFMIKE, sometimes if the alignment is way out, they do have to rotate the upper ball joints. The original ones only had two settings, they can only be bolted in place one of two positions that are 180 degrees apart. If you put the adjustable type of uppers on, there are more postions (four maybe, I don't remember) and better fine tuning is possible. I'd recommend Moog brand if you go with new ones. The upper ball joints are easier to replace than the lowers, but the lowers usually wear out faster. As far as turning the lower ball joints 180 degrees too, that's hogwash! They're pressed in and there is no way they can be adjusted differently. Turn them 180 degrees and it's still the same. Don't let them mess with the lowers unless they are worn out and need replaced.||||reg||1
Z||000004||RFMike||11-17-1999||07:23 AM||||Thanks for the replies.  I was getting the description for the work from the service manager, who doesn't inspire a lot of confidence in me.  I think the guys in the shop are pretty good, but I think the guy out in the front was hired because he smiles a lot.<BR>I was going to do this myself this winter, but I need to get it aligned now to maintain the tire warranty.  Rock  and a hard place.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||11-17-1999||03:02 PM||||I called the place where I have my alignments done. They told me if the upper ball joints need to be repositioned to bring it back to specs, they charge an extra $30 (US) of labor per side to do this above the normal $55 charged for a 4 wheel alignment. Maybe this will give you one idea of the costs.||||reg||1
Z||000006||RFMike||11-17-1999||08:32 PM||||Thanks, Batboy.  Gave it some thought on the way to and from work.  I hate to pay somebody else for something I planned to do, but this is one of those times.  I appreciate the info you guys gave me.<BR>The new skins are pretty decent.  I got the Dunlop DA60  P205/60R15 front and P215/ 60R15 back, per the original setup.  The grip is excellent, the ride is good, and the price was great.  These tires were rated the best all season performance radial by Car and Driver.  I have a set on a front wheel drive car, and they are great in snow, pretty good on ice.  Thought about Blizzaks, but decided if it was so bad that I coouldn't get through with the Dunlops, I should probably stay home.  When it snows, I'll post how they perform in the slippery stuff.<BR>Mike<p>[This message has been edited by RFMike (edited 11-17-1999).]||||reg||1
